{"observation": "cli/help", "command": ["/opt/gsb-fleet/workspaces/inst-34/verification/evaluation-rounds/1/verify/inst-34-004/a/checks/aggregate-cb6sy1f_/edgefleet", "help"], "exitCode": 0, "stdout": "usage: edgefleet [demo|version|aggregate --window-ms <milliseconds> [--partitions <n>]|help]

commands:
  demo       run a built-in fleet health demonstration
  version    print the edgefleet version
  aggregate  aggregate JSON events from standard input into fixed windows
  help       show this help

aggregate:
  edgefleet aggregate --window-ms <milliseconds> [--partitions <n>]

  --window-ms is a required positive signed 64-bit integer. Aggregation runs
  fully offline and never uses the current time; all timing comes from input.

  standard input is line-delimited JSON with two record types:
    {"type":"event","key":"sensor-a","time":1200,"value":5}
    {"type":"watermark","time":2000}
  time is a non-negative signed 64-bit millisecond timestamp, value is a
  signed 64-bit integer, and key is a non-empty string. Blank lines are
  ignored, but error line numbers count every physical input line.

  windows start at time zero, have the fixed length given by --window-ms
  and are left-closed, right-open: [start,end). Every valid event for a key
  in a window contributes to its count and sum; windows with no events do
  not produce output. Records may arrive out of order; window membership
  uses event time only.

  a watermark declares that events with time below it are late. Before the
  first watermark every valid event is accepted. After a watermark, every
  window whose end is less than or equal to the watermark is closed once
  and emitted as one JSON line with key,start,end,count,sum; the sum is a
  signed 64-bit integer. Closures are ordered by end ascending and then by
  key in UTF-8 byte order. Watermarks may jump or repeat, and an event at
  exactly the current watermark is still valid; events below the current
  watermark are skipped with a note on standard error.

  --partitions <n> optionally declares that input is merged from n
  independent partitions; n must be a positive signed 64-bit integer and
  invalid values are rejected before any input is read. In partition mode
  every event and watermark record must additionally carry an integer
  "partition" field in [0,n):
    {"type":"event","partition":0,"key":"sensor-a","time":1200,"value":5}
    {"type":"watermark","partition":1,"time":2000}
  partition only names the input source: events with the same key and
  window are still merged into one count and sum, and output records carry
  no partition field. Each partition advances its own watermark. No
  effective watermark exists until every partition has reported at least
  one watermark; until then no window closes and no event is late. From
  then on the effective watermark is the minimum of the per-partition
  values, so one partition racing ahead cannot close windows or discard
  events that another partition may still contribute to. Per-partition
  watermarks may repeat or jump forward but may never move backwards; a
  regression is a fatal error even when the overall minimum is unchanged.
  without --partitions the single-watermark behaviour above applies,
  including ignoring extra fields; --partitions 1 still requires every
  record to carry partition 0.

  at end of input the watermark is not advanced and still-open windows are
  not emitted. Malformed JSON, missing or mistyped fields, out-of-range
  integers or partitions, unknown record types, watermark regression, and
  window-end or cumulative-sum overflow print the line number and reason
  to standard error and exit non-zero; earlier output is retained and no
  later records are processed. Standard output contains window results
  only.
